:::oak-task {id="task-a"}

:::oak-heading {id="task-a-heading" level="2"}
Task A: Purpose and features of design specifications
:::

:::oak-paragraph {id="task-a-context"}
Below is a non-example of a lunchbox design specification. It is missing important details and does not clearly describe the product's purpose, user, or key features.
:::

:::oak-question {id="question-1"}
Improve this specification by adding missing information to make the description clearer.

:::oak-table {id="lunchbox-non-example"}
Specification point | Non-example | Your improvement
Purpose | Make the lunchbox. | ?
User | Anyone. | ?
Key features | It holds food and is a box. | ?
:::
:::
:::

:::oak-task {id="task-b"}

:::oak-heading {id="task-b-heading" level="2"}
Task B: Function and aesthetics in design specifications
:::

:::oak-question {id="question-2" number="1"}
You have already worked on the purpose, user, and key features of a lunchbox. Now, focus on improving the aesthetics, materials, and form.

Look at the following incomplete specification and improve it by adding details for these three elements.

:::oak-table {id="lunchbox-elements"}
Element | Consider | Your detail
Aesthetics | What should the lunchbox look like? (Consider colour, style, and design) | ?
Materials | What should the lunchbox be made of to ensure it’s durable and safe? | ?
Form | What shape should it be? How should it feel when carried? | ?
:::
:::

:::oak-question {id="question-3" number="2"}
For each part of your design specification: justify your design decisions, and explain how it would be tested.

